export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><em>T</em>he term <em>affordance</em> refers to a design element that provides operational hints to users.</p>\n<p>A classic example would be adding a metal plate on a door to suggest pushing or adding a door handle to suggest grabbing and turning. The purpose of affordances is to <strong>remove guesswork</strong>, smoothing the user experience.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/dc064ba938699cf97869.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/dc064ba938699cf97869.webp\" width=\"600\" height=\"389\" style=\"--source-width:309px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>The plate <em>affords </em>pushing while the handle <em>affords</em> pulling.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/81e1797ef271370ab687.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/81e1797ef271370ab687.webp\" width=\"645\" height=\"501\" style=\"--source-width:257px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>The handle affords pushing. It has a bright white color to catch your attention.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/6d70cec209bbe5d319c5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/6d70cec209bbe5d319c5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:257px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>The ramp affords jumping. The angle of the ramp also points toward a climbing edge.</p></td><td><p>Following this principle, we can design game props in ways that immediately inform players of their usage. There is a number of techniques we can employ to achieve such effects. One common technique is <strong>sculpting distinct and consistent shapes for interactive objects</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>On the flip side, we have <em>deny affordances:</em> objects that prevent players from accessing where we don’t want them to. It basically means “Hey, don’t go there,” or “don’t do that.”</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/84c01ce8758e3bbd5d57.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/84c01ce8758e3bbd5d57.webp\" width=\"631\" height=\"415\" style=\"--source-width:309px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/3cdea46994d32f1a245a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/3cdea46994d32f1a245a.webp\" width=\"1116\" height=\"623\" style=\"--source-width:309px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Using these two tools in tandem will lend a sense of certainty to your game levels. Before you know it, your players will be able to speed through your environments without ever stopping to think, “Huh, so where do I go now?”</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/2d4370250b7e18180568.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/2d4370250b7e18180568.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:257px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>You can add simple affordances during the block-out stage to outline your level.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
export const images = ["assets/concept-inline/2d4370250b7e18180568.webp", "assets/concept-inline/3cdea46994d32f1a245a.webp", "assets/concept-inline/6d70cec209bbe5d319c5.webp", "assets/concept-inline/81e1797ef271370ab687.webp", "assets/concept-inline/84c01ce8758e3bbd5d57.webp", "assets/concept-inline/dc064ba938699cf97869.webp"];
